# {{ display_name }}: Revit Model Context Protocol (MCP) Bridge Guide

This workspace includes an alpha, localhost-only **Model Context Protocol
(MCP)** bridge. It lets MCP clients read structured metadata from an active
Revit session through pyRevit Routes. Every tool is read-only.

> **Verification status:** automated contract tests cover the external Python
> server and the extension runtime, but this generated bridge has not been run
> in a live Revit/pyRevit session. Treat the documented endpoints and
> lifecycle behavior as unverified until a live check records evidence.

This file is generated and managed: `formwork render` rewrites it, and an edit
to it blocks the next render as a conflict. Put firm-specific notes in a
firm-owned file instead.

---

## 1. System architecture

Two components run on your workstation:

```
AI agent / client  <--stdio or HTTP-->  Python MCP server (port 3001)
                                                  |
                                                 HTTP
                                                  |
Autodesk Revit  <------------------->  pyRevit Routes ({{ routes_url }})
```

1. **pyRevit Routes extension (inside Revit)**
   - Lives in `extensions/{{ extension }}.extension/`: `startup.py` and the
     `lib/revit_mcp_bridge/` package.
   - Registers its routes under the API name `{{ namespace }}` using pyRevit's
     built-in Routes engine, so requests go to `{{ routes_url }}`.
   - Runs on the Revit main thread to read Revit API objects safely.
2. **Python MCP server (outside Revit)**
   - Lives in `servers/revit-mcp/`. It runs in its own local Python virtual
     environment and does not use Revit's embedded Python.
   - Binds to `http://127.0.0.1:3001` or standard input/output (stdio).
   - Translates MCP tool requests (for example `revit_project_info`) into
     Routes HTTP requests against `{{ routes_url }}` and returns normalized
     responses. Override the URL with the `REVIT_ROUTES_BASE_URL` environment
     variable; it must stay on loopback and end with the `{{ namespace }}`
     prefix.

